92186092422 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092422 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092422 #edb/ref 1045 "The Bivariate Normal Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092422 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186093234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092422 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092422 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "2f440ce7-75c4-5ffe-a946-16960f17a55a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092560 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092560 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X = [X_{1}, X_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $μ$ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n$μ = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the distribution of $Y = X_{1} - 2X_{2}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092560 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "695d4d94-877b-5df0-9f8f-05a7c0cd7954" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092560 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092579 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092579 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159689" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092579 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X = [X_{1}, X_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of $X_{1}$ and $X_{2}$ and their covariance are given by\n\n$σ_{1}^{2} = 10, σ_{2}^{2} = 5, σ_{12} = 5$.\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n$f(x) = \\frac{1}{k}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x - μ)^{T}Σ^{-1}(x - μ))$,\n\nwhere $μ$ is the mean vector and $Σ$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092579 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0707e3bd-3da9-5662-809b-216f2be6b97a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092580 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092580 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159694" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092580 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X = [X_{1}, X_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $μ$ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n$μ = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nLet $Y = X_{1} - 2X_{2}$. Calculate $P(Y \\ge 1)$.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.03$ | $0.04$ | $0.05$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.8$ | $0.7967$ | $0.7995$ | $0.8023$ |\n| $0.9$ | $0.8238$ | $0.8264$ | $0.8289$ |\n| $1.0$ | $0.8485$ | $0.8508$ | $0.8531$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092580 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "89c577b9-8c2b-5e60-9161-7d08d52dcc46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092580 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092581 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092581 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092581 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X = [X_{1}, X_{2}, X_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $μ$ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n$μ = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nLet $Y = X_{1} + 2X_{2} - X_{3}$. Find $P(Y < 1)$.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.05$ | $0.06$ | $0.07$ | $0.08$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.5$ | $0.7088$ | $0.7123$ | $0.7157$ | $0.7190$ |\n| $0.6$ | $0.7422$ | $0.7454$ | $0.7486$ | $0.7517$ |\n| $0.7$ | $0.7734$ | $0.7764$ | $0.7794$ | $0.7823$ |\n| $0.8$ | $0.8023$ | $0.8051$ | $0.8078$ | $0.8106$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092581 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "719754e3-0e30-5697-91d7-a36d391d48f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092581 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093234 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093234 #edb/ref 1061 42251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093234 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e60ad5d9-9bd3-5e95-8514-6ca6117c0c14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093234 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093235 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093235 #edb/ref 1061 42252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093235 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af6e2582-70c7-596f-8bcd-b192815cf772" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093235 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093236 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093236 #edb/ref 1061 42253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093236 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "93c0e956-bdbc-5097-ae5f-36a3027a1849" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093236 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093237 #edb/ref 1061 42254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093237 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eb1f1c89-4a73-5654-9bf4-8fbb33f576a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093237 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093238 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093238 #edb/ref 1061 42260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093238 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f937d23e-c522-5623-bb89-f42667039185" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093238 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093239 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093239 #edb/ref 1061 42318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093239 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "56eb632e-0a3e-5c4c-a62a-0984c2a4a72a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093239 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093240 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093240 #edb/ref 1061 42319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093240 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "35262d1e-3ec3-5c70-a2dc-cca3fc1c3e76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093240 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093241 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093241 #edb/ref 1061 42320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093241 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f16fc1c1-fc47-5b78-88b4-8c18145f6d33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093241 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093481 #edb/ref 1064 "Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T\n$$\n\nbe two (dependent) random variables. We say that $\\mathbf{X}$ is a **bivariate normal vector** with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ if the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{\\mid \\Sigma \\mid}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nμ = \\begin{bmatrix}μ_{1} \\\\ μ_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & σ_{12} \\\\ σ_{21} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & ρσ_{1}σ_{2} \\\\ ρσ_{1}σ_{2} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n- $\\sigma^2_1 = \\textrm{Var}[X_1]$ and $\\sigma^2_2 = \\textrm{Var}[X_2]$ are the variances of $X_1$ and $X_2$,\n- $\\sigma_{12} = \\sigma_{21} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2]$ is the covariance of $X_1$ and $X_2$,\n- $\\rho$ is the linear correlation coefficient of $X_1$ and $X_2$, and\n- $\\mid \\Sigma \\mid$ denotes the determinant of $\\Sigma$, the covariance matrix.\n\nA graph of a typical density function of a bivariate normal vector is depicted below.\n\n![](images/d1/d195358d6dc5b7b8a1080d53c774ee4289ce0f97c17de3bf45a52e86c6b20e29.png)\n\nThis surface is often referred to as having a \"bell shape.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093481 #edb/ref 1065 6997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093481 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cfec4e77-fdaf-5a78-9be4-8fdc89abc5bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093481 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093482 #edb/ref 1064 "Earlier, we compared the PDF of $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$, given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}1}/2} ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2} (x-\\mu) ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1} (x-\\mu) \\right)\n$$\n\nand the joint PDF of a bivariate normal distribution\n\n$$\n\\mathbf X = [X_1,X_2]^T\n$$\n\nwith mean vector $\\boldsymbol\\mu$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$, given by\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}2}/2} {\\color{blue} \\mid \\Sigma \\mid}^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,{\\color{blue}\\Sigma}^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nWe can generalize this for an arbitrary number of variables ${\\color{red}p}$, as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_p) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}p}/2} {\\color{blue} \\mid \\Sigma \\mid}^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,{\\color{blue}\\Sigma}^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_p)$ is called the **multivariate normal** density function. A random vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf X = [X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_p]^T\n$$\n\nwith this joint density function is said to follow a **multivariate normal distribution**.\n\nSimilar to the bivariate case, we can consider linear combinations of coordinates of multivariate normal random vectors:\n\n> *If $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k]^T$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$, then the random variable $Y$, defined as $Y= \\mathbf c^T \\mathbf X = \\sum_{i=1}^k c_i X_i$ is a normally distributed random variable, where $\\mathbf c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= c^{T}μ, \\\\\nVar[Y] &= c^{T}Σc\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression for the variance is a quadratic form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093482 #edb/ref 1065 6998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093482 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e2ce75d0-2db0-5254-880f-d1042f119f29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093482 #edb/ref 1067 "The Multivariate Normal Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093483 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a normally distributed random variable $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)^2}\n$$\n\nThis probability density function can be written as\n\n$$\nf(x) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}1}/2} ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2} (x-\\mu) ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1} (x-\\mu) \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compare this to the density function of a bivariate normal vector. First, we rewrite the joint density $f(x_1,x_2)$ of the bivariate normal distribution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x_{1}, x_{2}) &= \\frac{1}{2π\\sqrt{\\mid Σ \\mid}}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X - μ)^{T}Σ^{-1}(X - μ)) \\\\\n&= \\left.(2π)^{-1} \\mid Σ\\right|^{-1/2}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X - μ)^{T}Σ^{-1}(X - μ)) \\\\\n&= \\left.(2π)^{-2/2} \\mid Σ\\right|^{-1/2}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X - μ)^{T}Σ^{-1}(X - μ))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy comparing the expressions for $f(x)$ and $f(x_1,x_2)$, we see that the *bivariate* normal distribution can be considered as a generalization of the *normal distribution* from the ${\\color{red}1}$-dimensional case (when we have only one variable, $x$) to the ${\\color{red}2}$-dimensional case (when we have two variables, $x_1$ and $x_2$).\n\nThis analogy will be particularly helpful when extending the bivariate normal distribution to more than two random variables." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093483 #edb/ref 1065 7016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093483 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "198d2a80-9efc-509c-8852-d560b1203524" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093483 #edb/ref 1067 "Normal Distribution vs. Bivariate Normal Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093484 #edb/ref 1064 "A linear combination of coordinates of a bivariate normal random vector generates a (one-dimensional) normal distribution:\n\n> *If $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$, given by*\n> $μ = \\begin{bmatrix}μ_{1} \\\\ μ_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & σ_{12} \\\\ σ_{21} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$,\n> *then the random variable $Y$, defined as*\n> $Y= aX_1 + bX_2$\n> *is a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b$. Furthermore, we have that*\n> $E[Y]|= aμ_{1} + bμ_{2},; Var[Y]|= a^{2}σ_{1}^{2} + b^{2}σ_{2}^{2} + 2abσ_{12}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093484 #edb/ref 1065 7017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093484 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "62a410d8-f059-5273-ba3a-15646c153a7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093484 #edb/ref 1067 "Linear Combinations of Normal Random Variables From Bivariate Distributions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093485 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider a bivariate normal vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T\n$$\n\nwith the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{\\mid \\Sigma \\mid}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere the mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ are given by\n\n$$\nμ = \\begin{bmatrix}μ_{1} \\\\ μ_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & σ_{12} \\\\ σ_{21} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & ρσ_{1}σ_{2} \\\\ ρσ_{1}σ_{2} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMost of the parameters of the distribution can be interpreted geometrically on a corresponding graph of the probability density function:\n\n- The mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ gives the $xy$-coordinates of the peak.\n- The correlation coefficient $\\rho$ defines how much the bell is squeezed towards the plane $y=x{:}$ when $\\rho \\to 0$, the horizontal cross-sections are almost circular, whilewhen $\\rho \\to 1$, the shape becomes more and more squeezed towards the plane $y=x$.\n\n![](images/5b/5bd69db417134cccedd5af45cb56fb44f27925ba5a8470f3b5622f0856ed855d.png)\n\n- The variances $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ represent how much the curve is spread out along the principal axes $x_1=\\mu_1$ and $x_2=\\mu_2{:}$when $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are small, the bell shape is more \"pointy,\" whilefor larger $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$, the bell shape is more \"flat.\"\n\nThe level curves of the surface have equations of the form\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu) = \\textrm{const}\n$$\n\nwhich represent ellipses centered at $(\\mu_1,\\mu_2)$.\n\n![](images/1f/1f6b5643b7ab0568faa0b6e16b55a11482000b09d1b5862158d4ac9649281bb7.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093485 #edb/ref 1065 7018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093485 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0f898055-8522-58fc-8460-b4e13ed3afb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093485 #edb/ref 1067 "Interpretation of Parameters in a Bivariate Normal Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
